{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/periodic-pattern-transfer.md", "content": "# Periodic-pattern transfer from sparse marker grids

A sparse input may encode a palette/order and phase for a periodic output, rather than a local fill operation. Read the output demo as a 2D sequence: identify the minimal period, row-to-row phase shift, and top-left phase. Verify exact candidate mappings cellwise; common alternatives include `(r+c) mod p` and `(c-r) mod p`, which may look alike but diverge on asymmetric patterns.

Do not transfer a periodic sequence from one compelling demo unless the same role-level rule reproduces every example. No-op pairs and outputs with apparently different palettes/periods are evidence that the operation may be conditional or that the current abstraction is wrong. Following an incorrect submission, binary feedback cannot pinpoint phase versus palette errors. Avoid arbitrary variant cycling; request a discriminating demo while available, and if capped, make one best-supported choice rather than repeat rejected outputs.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/periodic-pattern-transfer.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/singleton-demo-transfer.md", "content": "# Singleton outputs and cross-geometry transfer

In a stream where examples differ sharply in grid structure, one demo may yield a tiny or singleton output. Treat that as evidence about that input's transformation, not as a default output shape or palette for the test. Before transferring, compare all examples by dimensions, structural roles, and output mapping; identify a candidate shared rule that explains why outputs differ and replay it completely. A demo request is not a substitute for extracting its information: log what its output rules in or out, and request another only when competing models make different predictions. After a rejection, binary feedback cannot localize the error; revisit output shape and trigger alongside cell mapping instead of repeating the singleton guess.

This session's 1×1 output was color 4 for a 28×14 patterned demo, but provided no validated mapping to the distinct 27×27 periodic test. The subsequent 1×1 [4] submission was incorrect, confirming that copying the demo output was unsupported. No solution rule was established here.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/singleton-demo-transfer.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/attempt-cap-and-retry-gate.md", "content": "# Attempt-cap handling and retry gate

In strict one-action puzzle interfaces, the protocol remains binding after the puzzle ends. When feedback says the instance is over—especially after exhausting the attempt cap—respond with exactly `{"action":"finish"}`. An empty assistant response or another `submit` is not a valid terminal action.

After a wrong submission, binary feedback gives no location information. Do not respond with a cosmetic or arbitrary variant (for example, changing only a suspected region's color). State the proposed model correction internally and test it against the complete demonstrations, including unchanged pairs and preserved cells. Only submit when the revised rule has evidence and reproduces all examples. If ambiguity remains and a demo is available, request one only to resolve a specific competing hypothesis; once demos are exhausted, further unsupported guesses compound cost and risk consuming the cap.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/attempt-cap-and-retry-gate.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/heterogeneous-demo-role-reconciliation.md", "content": "# Heterogeneous-demo role reconciliation

When demonstrations differ sharply in dimensions, palettes, and object structures, log the exact changed-cell mask and the roles of each color/object separately for every pair. A shared color numeral or visual resemblance is not evidence of a shared transformation. Replay each candidate rule against every complete demo, including untouched cells and identity examples; reject rules that fail any pair. Transfer only a role-level invariant supported across examples. If none is supported, request another demo only when you can name competing hypotheses and the concrete observation that would discriminate them. Avoid speculative large edits based on one salient pattern.

In the reviewed instance, one example changed a single vertical stripe to color 8 while another changed one background-colored column adjacent to a marker into 8, leaving the marker/object untouched. These observations did not establish a common rule for the test grid, which had a broad color-6 pattern and color-2 marker stripes. The useful lesson is to reconcile changed masks and roles before transferring, not to infer a task rule from palette numerals or one visual resemblance.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/heterogeneous-demo-role-reconciliation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/cross-demo-generalization.md", "content": "# Cross-demo generalization under diverse examples

Continual-ARC demos can vary sharply in grid size, palette, and the seed's shape or location while still instantiating an abstract common operation. Do not seek one literal template that can be copied unchanged from the first example. For each pair, record (1) background and marker roles, (2) exact seed footprint including background-valued cells, (3) output additions and any removals, (4) placement/alignment rule, and (5) whether unrelated cells are preserved. Compare these role-level summaries across every pair.

Validate competing hypotheses by predicting the complete output for each demo. Similar-looking seed colors are not proof that every marker receives a stamp; check changed-cell masks and unchanged regions. If no single rule explains all examples, request another demo while budget remains rather than submitting a candidate that only resembles one pair. Once the rule is supported, apply it mechanically to the test input and audit the serialized grid separately.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/cross-demo-generalization.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/sparse-grid-demo-lesson.md", "content": "# Sparse-grid line completion lesson

In a sparse grid with scattered same-color marks, a demo may reveal that only specific aligned interior cells are painted a new color. The useful analysis is cellwise: record changed coordinates, separate changed background cells from original marks, then test row and column runs and their endpoints. Do not infer that every possible aligned pair is filled; verify every qualifying and non-qualifying run in the example. Transfer the smallest rule that exactly reproduces the full demo, preserving all original cells unless the output demonstrates replacement. In the reviewed instance, the test transformation was to paint background cells lying strictly between vertically aligned 0 markers using color 3, with marks and other cells preserved. First demo request gave no validation of this rule; the second demonstrated inserted 3s in line interiors. Exact output feedback confirmed the derived test answer.

Treat this as an evidence-backed pattern, not a universal ARC rule: obtain/compare examples to establish axis, endpoint, and color conditions before applying.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/sparse-grid-demo-lesson.md"}
